course: dictionary
lesson: 9958
title: Calling Dad in Ukrainian
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: In Ukrainian, you change the end of a name when you call someone. This is called the calling form, or vocative. English does not do this, so it may feel strange at first.
explain: The ordinary word for dad is «tato». When you shout to him, you say «Tatu!», which sounds like "TAH-too" and means "Dad!". Notice the ending changed from -o to -u.
explain: The pattern works for other names too: MAma becomes MAmo!, and BAbusya becomes BAbusyu!. Find the ending, change it, and you can call anyone the right way.
fact: «Tatu!» is the calling form of «tato», the Ukrainian word for dad.
fact: «Tatu» is not an English word, so it is a loan word you only meet in a Ukrainian context.
fact: In Ukrainian, the calling form changes the end of a name, which is called the vocative case.
fact: «Mamo!» means «Mum!», the calling form of «mama».
fact: «Tatu» also means three in Swahili, as in the count «moja, mbili, tatu» (one, two, three), and it means armadillo in Portuguese.
exercise: In Ukrainian, what does «Tatu!» mean when you shout it? | Three! | *Dad, when you call him | Dad! | Armadillo!
exercise: In Swahili counting, «moja, mbili, tatu». What is «tatu»? | Two | One | Four | *Three
exercise: What is the calling form of «tato»? | *tatu | tato | tatum | tata
test: The calling form of «MAma» is: | MAmi! | MAmu! | *MAmo! | MAma
test: In Portuguese, «tatu» means: | Three | Dad | *Armadillo | A pop singer
exam: What is the calling form of «BAbusya»? | *BAbusyu! | BAbusya! | BAbusyo! | BAbusyi!
